---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ipsas/introduction-cash-basis.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Purpose of This Introduction & the IPSASB's Mandate" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Two Bases, One Standard-Setter" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Adoption, Jurisdictional Autonomy & the Compliance Threshold" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Path Forward – Encouraging Transition to Accrual Basis" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Related Standards & What Comes Next" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="The Introduction to the Cash Basis IPSAS provides the preamble and background for the 2017 revised Cash Basis IPSAS, explaining jurisdictional autonomy, the binary compliance threshold for Part 1, and the path forward to accrual accounting."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 – PURPOSE OF THIS INTRODUCTION & THE IPSASB'S MANDATE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Purpose of This Introduction &amp; the IPSASB's Mandate</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A short preamble, not a Standard in its own right</div>
      <div class="principle">This Introduction is a BRIEF preamble to the Cash Basis IPSAS – it carries no numbered paragraphs, no requirements, and no authoritative force of its own. Its role is purely to set context before the actual Standard (<em>Financial Reporting Under the Cash Basis of Accounting</em>) begins on the following page.</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>The IPSASB develops accounting standards for public sector entities – International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSAS)</span>
        <span>Its stated goal: achieving CONSISTENT and COMPARABLE financial information ACROSS jurisdictions</span>
        <span>It strongly ENCOURAGES governments and national standard-setters to engage with its Exposure Drafts and Consultation Papers during development</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 – TWO BASES, ONE STANDARD-SETTER ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Two Bases, One Standard-Setter</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A deliberately dual-track body of literature</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The IPSASB issues IPSAS covering BOTH the CASH BASIS of accounting AND the ACCRUAL BASIS – this Introduction sits at the head of the cash-basis track specifically, distinct from the 51-Standard accrual-basis body of literature covered elsewhere in this library (IPSAS 1–51).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Why a cash-basis Standard exists at all</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Many governments and public sector entities, particularly in developing jurisdictions, report on a cash basis as a practical matter – often as a stepping stone toward eventual accrual adoption. Rather than leaving this reporting basis entirely unregulated, the IPSASB provides a dedicated Standard so that even cash-basis financial statements can achieve a baseline of consistency, comparability, and credibility.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 – ADOPTION, JURISDICTIONAL AUTONOMY & THE COMPLIANCE THRESHOLD ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Adoption, Jurisdictional Autonomy &amp; the Compliance Threshold</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Improving quality and comparability – while respecting sovereignty</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Adoption of IPSAS is expected to improve BOTH the quality and comparability of public sector financial information globally. At the same time, the IPSASB explicitly RECOGNIZES the right of governments and national standard-setters to establish their OWN accounting standards and guidelines for their jurisdictions – this Standard is offered as an important step forward, not a mandate.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The all-or-nothing compliance rule</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Financial statements may be described as complying with this IPSAS ONLY IF they comply with ALL the requirements of PART 1</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">A genuinely important threshold: partial compliance, or compliance with only the Standard's ENCOURAGED disclosures (Part 2), does not entitle an entity to claim conformity with the Cash Basis IPSAS. This mirrors the accrual-basis IPSAS's own "unreserved compliance statement" concept (see IPSAS 1 and IPSAS 33 in this library) – compliance is binary, not a matter of degree.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 – THE PATH FORWARD: ENCOURAGING TRANSITION TO ACCRUAL BASIS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Path Forward – Encouraging Transition to Accrual Basis</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">This Standard is framed as a stepping stone, not a destination</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The IPSASB explicitly ENCOURAGES governments to progress from cash-basis reporting to the ACCRUAL BASIS of accounting, and to harmonize national requirements with the accrual-basis IPSAS body of literature (IPSAS 1–51, covered extensively elsewhere in this library) – the Cash Basis IPSAS is positioned as an interim improvement, not a permanent alternative.</div>
      <div class="note">For entities planning an eventual transition, the Introduction points to a further IPSASB resource: <strong>Study 14, <em>Transition to the Accrual Basis of Accounting: Guidance for Governments and Government Entities</em></strong> – a practical implementation guide sitting outside the Handbook's numbered Standards, but part of the IPSASB's broader publication suite alongside Recommended Practice Guidelines (RPGs) and Studies.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 – RELATED STANDARDS & WHAT COMES NEXT ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Related Standards &amp; What Comes Next</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Where this Introduction fits in the Handbook, and what follows immediately after</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.8fr;"><span>Document</span><span>Relationship</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.8fr;"><span>Financial Reporting Under the Cash Basis of Accounting (Part 1)</span><span>The actual Standard this Introduction precedes – mandatory requirements for claiming compliance</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.8fr;"><span>Financial Reporting Under the Cash Basis of Accounting (Part 2)</span><span>Encouraged additional disclosures – voluntary, does not affect the compliance statement</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.8fr;"><span>Preface to IPSAS</span><span>The broader introduction to the ENTIRE IPSASB publication suite (both bases), covered separately in this library</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.8fr;"><span>Conceptual Framework</span><span>Applies conceptually across both bases, though written primarily with accrual-basis reporting in mind</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.8fr;"><span>IPSAS 33</span><span>Governs first-time TRANSITION to accrual basis IPSAS – the destination this Introduction points toward (&sect;4)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.8fr;"><span>Study 14</span><span>Practical transition guidance, referenced directly by this Introduction (&sect;4)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
